# This app directory is force-pushed VERBATIM into a fresh Gitea repo by
# scripts/50-seed-gitea-repos.sh (push_repo: cp -a -> git init -> git add -A -> push -f).
#
# The root .gitignore CANNOT protect that repo: its build-output rules are `apps/**/`-anchored,
# and at the fresh repo's root there is no `apps/` prefix left to match. Measured 2026-09-07:
# 845 of 905 staged files are ignored by the OUTER repo and 3 by the fresh-repo anchoring.
#
# gitignore(5): "these patterns match relative to the location of the .gitignore file" — so a file
# HERE anchors identically in both repos. That is the whole reason it exists; it is NOT redundant.
# Every .dockerignore in this tree excludes .gitignore, so this changes no image context.

# Unanchored: .NET writes obj/ and bin/ per PROJECT, so they appear at any depth, and the root
# .gitignore's `apps/**/obj/` has the same any-depth semantics.
obj/
bin/
TestResults/

# ⚠️ SECRETS — the SAME mechanism, the worse consequence. The seeded repo is created
# `"private":false` (50-seed-gitea-repos.sh) and FORCE-PUSHED, and the root .gitignore's secret
# patterns are UNANCHORED, so they protect the outer repo at any depth and are ABSENT here.
# Nothing in this tree matches them today (verified), so this excludes nothing legitimate — it is
# here so that the day someone drops a kubeconfig beside an app, it does not become public.
# Mirrors the root's own lines, negation included.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig
*.pem
*.key
# ⚠️ MIRROR THE ROOT'S testdata NEGATION. gitignore precedence gives the DEEPER file priority, so
# the `*.key` above OVERRIDES the root's `!**/testdata/**/*.key` (.gitignore:18) — a deliberate
# carve-out, present since the scaffold commit, so crypto/TLS test fixtures can be committed.
# WITHOUT this line the per-app file does not merely fail to add protection at fresh-repo root; it
# REMOVES protection in the OUTER repo, and `git add` on a new testdata/*.key silently does nothing.
# MEASURED before this line: apps/go/gowebapp/testdata/ca.key was ignored by *.key here, while a
# root-level testdata/root.key was correctly negated by the root — the discriminating control.
!**/testdata/**/*.key
